# Defining functions and predicates, including by recurrence relations.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# Besides typing expressions, you can name them. A definition whose body is a
# term defines a function; one whose body is a formula defines a predicate. Both
# are written with :=

f(x) := x'          # a function: its body is a term
p(x) := x' = 0      # a predicate: its body is a formula

# A definition is used by applying it, and the application is expanded during
# normalization:

n f(Y)
n p(Y)

# Definitions may also be recursive, in which case they are indexed by an offset
# written in brackets after the name. A recurrence relation needs a base case,
# giving a fixed offset, and a step case, referring to a smaller offset:

h[0](x) := 1             # base case
h[n](x) := x ^ h[n-1](x) # step case

# Applying it at a concrete offset unfolds the recurrence down to the base case.
# Seven exclusive-ors of x with 1 come out as the complement of x:

n h[7](x)

# So h[7](x) is not everywhere nonzero, since it is zero when x is 1:

n all x h[7](x) != 0

# The head of a definition may carry a type, which fixes the Boolean algebra the
# definition works over. This pair alternates between 0 and 1 with the parity of
# the offset, in the sbf Boolean algebra:

g[0](y):sbf := 0
g[n](y):sbf := g[n-1](y)'

n g[4](y:sbf)
n g[5](y:sbf)

# Predicates take offsets in exactly the same way:

q[0](x) := x = 0
q[n](x) := q[n-1](x) && x != 1

n q[3](Y)

# Definitions live in their own store, separate from the history. To list them,
# or print one by position:

defs
defs 1

# Two rules are worth knowing, because breaking either is the usual way a
# definition goes wrong.

# First, a relative offset in the body has to be bound by an offset on the head.
# Without one there is nothing to count down from, so the definition below is
# rejected as it is entered rather than looping later:

r[0](x) := 1
r[n](x) := r[n-1](x)
f2(x) := o1[n] = r[n](x)

# Second, a recursive definition has to make progress towards its base case.
# This one rewrites to itself forever, which is detected and reported instead of
# hanging:

f3(x) := f3(x)'
n f3(1)

quit
